BFSOS kernel maintenance helper
===============================

Kernel packages use a two-phase update policy:

1. Before package replacement, preserve the current/known-good rollback kernel.
2. Install and generate the replacement kernel/initramfs, mark it pending, and
   atomically regenerate GRUB.
3. On the first successful boot into the pending kernel,
   bfs-kernel-finalize.service marks it known-good and removes older artifacts
   while retaining the immediately previous known-good kernel.

A second update of the same kernel flavor is refused while an earlier update is
still pending boot verification. This prevents an unbooted kernel from evicting
the last known-good rollback entry.


Legacy kernel naming migration (r484)
-----------------------------------
Install bfs-kernel-maintenance 1.0-5 first. A migration does NOT uninstall
legacy packages or touch the currently booted kernel. Run, on a disposable
VM with verified disk/UEFI-variable backups:

  sudo bfs-kernel-migrate status lts
  sudo bfs-kernel-migrate prepare lts --yes

For an old 7.x package named linux, prepare **mainline first** so that the
new LTS can take the linux package name:

  sudo bfs-kernel-migrate prepare mainline --yes

The old 7.x pkgutils record is relabelled linux-legacy-mainline, retaining
ownership of its old boot/module files. New mainline is separate. For LTS,
only the shared boot/vmlinuz-lts alias is disowned in the old pkg record.
No files are deleted. Preserve the old kernel until the newly named kernel
has booted and passed an independent recovery/rollback test. Inspect the
backup path printed by the helper. Do NOT use pkgrm on the old package until
legacy ownership of shared paths and bootability have been reviewed.

The helper is intentionally a *prepare-only* operation. It does not claim
boot verification, GRUB validity, or automatically delete a legacy package.
